Scabies
Paediatric & Neonatal
Nocturnal intense pruritus, Interdigital linear burrows, Multiple affected family members

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: PDN-044
Reason for Visit: Intense, unremitting, generalized itching preventing sleep for the entire family, requiring evaluation to rule out severe atopic dermatitis flare or widespread allergic reaction.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient, siblings, and parents
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage documented multiple family members with severe, sleep-depriving pruritus and a spreading papular rash that is completely unresponsive to antihistamines. Immediate after-hours assessment was required to diagnose the highly contagious parasitic infestation, rule out secondary bacterial impetiginization, and initiate simultaneous household treatment prior to the next in-hours period to halt further community transmission.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "Our whole family has been scratching ourselves raw every single night, and the hydrocortisone cream from the pharmacy does absolutely nothing."
History of Present Illness: The patient presents with intense, unremitting pruritus that significantly and reliably worsens at night, causing severe sleep disruption for the past two weeks. The mother reports a history of identical intensely itchy symptoms in multiple close family members living in the house. There is a complete lack of any significant symptom relief utilizing standard over-the-counter emollients or mild topical steroids. The rash is generalized, but explicitly localizes heavily to the wrists, axillae, and umbilicus.
Functional Status: Deeply exhausted from chronic sleep deprivation, constantly scratching.
Social/Safety Concerns: Extremely high risk of contagious transmission; severe household sleep deprivation.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 110/70 | HR: 95 | Temp: 36.8C | RR: 18 | SpO2: 100% | Wt: 35 Kg
General: Fidgety, visibly tired, excoriated skin visible on arms and neck.
Physical Exam: Cutaneous examination reveals visible, fine, linear grayish burrows distinctively located in the interdigital web spaces of the hands and volar aspects of the wrists. There are erythematous papules and widespread excoriations strictly localizing to the flexor surfaces of the wrists, axillae, and umbilicus. The rash explicitly spares the head and neck. There is no honey-colored crusting or fluctuance to indicate severe secondary bacterial impetiginization. The diagnostic tetrad of nocturnal intense pruritus, interdigital linear burrows, and multiple affected family members is explicitly confirmed.
Environmental Observations: All family members present are actively scratching.
Medication Reconciliation: Oral antihistamines and topical hydrocortisone 1% utilized with zero clinical effect.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Scabies - Status: Active household infestation
Differentials:

Severe Atopic Dermatitis - Ruled out by the sudden onset in previously healthy individuals, the simultaneous involvement of the entire family, and the presence of pathognomonic interdigital burrows.

Bed Bug Bites - Ruled out by the generalized distribution and the presence of fine burrows; bed bug bites classically present in linear groups of three ("breakfast, lunch, and dinner") on exposed skin only.

Papular Urticaria - Ruled out by the lack of response to systemic antihistamines and the specific predilection for the axillae, umbilicus, and interdigital spaces rather than exposed extensor surfaces.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: High (Highly contagious household and community transmission)

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The acute presentation of severe, unrelenting nocturnal pruritus affecting an entire household mandated an immediate after-hours clinical assessment. Telephone triage accurately identified a highly contagious, sleep-depriving outbreak. Objective clinical evaluation definitively confirmed a Sarcoptes scabiei infestation by visualizing the pathognomonic interdigital burrows, explicitly ruling out allergic dermatoses. Delaying this assessment would have subjected the family to continued agonizing pruritus, severe sleep deprivation, and increased the risk of secondary bacterial skin infections from relentless scratching. Immediate intervention secured the diagnosis and allowed for the critical, simultaneous prescription of topical scabicides for the entire household, abruptly halting the chain of transmission.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Prescribed topical Permethrin 5% cream. Directed application to the entire body from the neck down, to be left on overnight (8-14 hours) and washed off in the morning.
Positive Behavioral Change: The entire family must apply the cream simultaneously tonight and engage in a "laundry party" where all worn clothes, towels, and bedsheets are stripped and placed in hot water. By transforming the overwhelming chore of environmental decontamination into a structured, simultaneous family activity, the clinician ensures absolute compliance with the environmental control required to prevent immediate re-infestation, guaranteeing the medical treatment succeeds.
Home-Based Services (Optional): None.
DME/Safety (Optional): None.
Follow-up: Routine GP review not required unless live mites or new burrows appear after 14 days. A second dose of Permethrin must be applied by all members in exactly 7 days.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Routine medical follow-up only. Call the regular GP if the excoriated skin becomes hot, red, swollen, or starts discharging yellow pus (indicating secondary bacterial infection).

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: The intense itching is an allergic reaction to the dead mites and will persist for up to 2 to 4 weeks even after the mites are successfully killed.
Clinical Course: The Permethrin cream kills the mites tonight. A second application in 7 days is strictly required to kill any newly hatched eggs. The itch will not stop immediately.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Return to your GP if the scratched skin becomes bright red, hot to the touch, or oozes yellow pus.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: Microscopic mites have burrowed into the top layer of the skin. The prescription cream is a targeted insecticide that will kill them. The severe itching is an allergic reaction to the mites, which takes weeks to fade even after the mites are dead. Antihistamines can now be used to help sleep.
Actionable Steps: Apply the cream from the jawline down to the soles of the feet, including under the fingernails. Wash all bedding and clothing worn in the last 72 hours in hot water and dry on high heat. Repeat the exact cream application in 7 days.

